11:© The Author(s) 2025
12:
13:Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is a highly heterogeneous disorder, with notable variations among cases in structural brain abnormalities. To address this heterogeneity, our study aimed to delineate OCD subtypes based on individualized gray matter morphological differences. We recruited 100 untreated, /uniFB01 rst-episode OCD patients and 106 healthy controls for structural imaging scans. Utilizing normative models of gray matter volume, we identi /uniFB01 ed subtypes based on individual morphological abnormalities. Sensitivity analyses were conducted to validate the reproducibility of clustering outcomes. To gain deeper insights into the connectomic and molecular underpinnings of structural brain abnormalities in the identi /uniFB01 ed subtypes, we investigated their associations with normal brain network architecture and the distribution of neurotransmitter receptors/transporters. Our /uniFB01 ndings revealed two distinct OCD subtypes exhibiting divergent patterns of structural brain abnormalities. Sensitivity analysis results con /uniFB01 rmed the robustness of the identi /uniFB01 ed subtypes. Subtype 1 displayed signi /uniFB01 cantly increased gray matter volume in regions including the frontal gyrus, precuneus, insula, hippocampus, parahippocampal gyrus, amygdala, and temporal gyrus, while subtype 2 exhibited decreased gray matter volume in the frontal gyrus, precuneus, insula, superior parietal gyrus, temporal gyrus, and fusiform gyrus. When considering all patients collectively, structural brain abnormalities nulli /uniFB01 ed. The identi /uniFB01 ed subtypes were characterized by divergent disease epicenters. Speci /uniFB01 cally, subtype 1 showed disease epicenters in the middle frontal gyrus, while subtype 2 displayed disease epicenters in the striatum, thalamus and hippocampus. Furthermore, structural brain abnormalities in these subtypes displayed distinct associations with neurotransmitter receptors/transporters. The identi /uniFB01 ed subtypes offer novel insights into nosology and the heterogeneous nature of OCD.
